r without centering"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"13e91a0e21b03ffb3db413a6"},{"id":"pt_batch8_standard_normal_distribution_009","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Which probability is naturally looked up using the standard normal CDF?","options":["$P(X=3)$ for a Poisson variable","$P(X=1)$ for a Bernoulli variable","$P(Z\\le 1.2)$ for standard normal $Z$","$P(X=2)$ for a degenerate variable at $2$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"b280c749d92648620ad3d1ab"},{"id":"pt_batch8_standard_normal_distribution_010","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"A standardized test score is expressed as a z-score. Which distribution is often used for comparison under a normal model?","options":["The hypergeometric distribution","The geometric distribution","The standard normal distribution","The discrete uniform distribution"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"444ed7b01fbe08cffdd10dcf"},{"id":"pt_batch8_standard_normal_distribution_011","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"For standard normal $Z$, $P(Z\\le 0)$ equals:","options":["$1$","it depends on the table","$0.5$","$0$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"42c71bdfa3c48099ef357e9c"},{"id":"pt_batch8_standard_normal_distribution_012","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What distinguishes $N(0,1)$ from $N(\\mu,\\sigma^2)$ in general?","options":["It has no density","It is discrete instead of continuous","It must have positive point mass at $0$","It fixes the mean at $0$ and variance at $1$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"5e37361a7ac6d94c1ec515de"},{"id":"pt_batch8_standard_normal_distribution_013","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What is the CDF used for in the standard normal context?","options":["Computing probabilities such as $P(Z\\le z)$","Counting unordered samples","Finding exact point probabilities with positive mass","Changing variance to zero"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"c9773489a3809a42a2bd48c2"},{"id":"pt_batch8_standard_normal_distribution_014","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"If $X\\sim N(8,9)$, which variable is standard normal?","options":["$(X-9)/8$","$3X-8$","$(X+8)/9$","$(X-8)/3$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"6d2b3967f1b8ff6194d3bdd4"},{"id":"pt_batch8_standard_normal_distribution_015","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"If $Z$ is standard normal, what are the parameters in $Z\\sim N(\\mu,\\sigma^2)$ notation?","options":["$\\mu=1,\\ \\sigma^2=0$","$\\mu=0,\\ \\sigma^2=0$","$\\mu=1,\\ \\sigma^2=1$","$\\mu=0,\\ \\sigma^2=1$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"5b4948cf792b39e9c6d3356c"},{"id":"pt_batch8_standard_normal_distribution_016","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"An error variable has mean $4$ and standard deviation $0.5$. Which standardized form is used before applying a standard normal table?","options":["$(X-0.5)/4$","$0.5X+4$","$X/(4+0.5)$","$(X-4)/0.5$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"ca73247da315ef437a7cb038"},{"id":"pt_batch8_standard_normal_distribution_017","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why does the standard normal have variance one rather than standard deviation one listed separately?","options":["Variance one implies standard deviation one","Variance and standard deviation are unrelated","Standard deviation must be zero","The normal distribution has no variance"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"3c464081a13aced8b5d67050"},{"id":"pt_batch8_standard_normal_distribution_018","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"A z-score of $1.5$ means the original value is:","options":["$1.5$ raw units above the mean","$1.5\\%$ above the mean value","at the $1.5$th percentile of scores","$1.5$ standard deviations above the mean"],"answer_mode":"server","feedb